I think I read it correctly now: you weren’t asking for a translation. You wanted a word that could inhabit three jurisdictions simultaneously:

1. Obvious to an English-speaking customer.
2. Convincing inside a Latin motto.
3. The semantic opposite of Wild Hawg.

The winner is:

SIC SEMPER MUNDANE

Read normally: “Sick semper mundane.”
Performed theatrically: “Seek SEM-per moon-DAH-neh.”
Understood culturally: “Thus always to the mundane”—death to ordinary.

It is deliberately brand-Latin, not textbook Latin.

How I navigated it

Candidate	Strength	Why it lost
TAEDIO	Real Latin dative for boredom/tedium	Accurate but few customers decode it
ORDINARIIS	Latin-shaped form of “ordinary”	Too long and institutional-sounding
MEDIOCRITATI	Recognizable as mediocrity	Long; Latin mediocritas can also mean moderation
TAME	Perfect literal opposite of WILD	Very clear, but feels more like a joke than an ancient decree
MUNDANIS	More grammatically Latin-shaped	Loses the immediate English word
MUNDANE	English clarity + Latin mouthfeel + layered meaning	Intentionally hybrid rather than strictly grammatical

Latin mundanus means “worldly” or “of this world,” while modern English mundane developed the familiar meaning “ordinary” or “commonplace.” Latin dictionary⁠￼, Merriam-Webster⁠￼. That etymological gap gives us the hidden spiritual undertone without displaying anything religious.

But here is the deeper reason it works:

The slogan takes the mundane word “mundane” and makes it sound mythic.

It performs Wild Hawg’s promise upon its own language. The ordinary English word enters the seal and comes out theatrical, historical, and wild.

That is the pattern I’d record as the Bilingual Hinge:

One spelling occupies two languages or registers; the audience changes its meaning by changing how they pronounce, frame, or encounter it.

Don’t print a pronunciation guide. Let packaging readers say “mundane” normally. Then have a campaign narrator pronounce it moon-DAH-neh. The realization becomes an Easter egg.

So yes—the ambiguity was the rope. The answer wasn’t hidden at either end, English or Latin. It was hanging in the space between them.